From: mpc-pi
Subject: [PATCH] mpc2000xl: make MIDI panel-key injection atomic

A panel key event is TWO bytes on the V53 SCU - 0x84 or 0x85, then the keycode
- and the receiver has no way to resynchronise if it is handed one of them.
Every other panel message goes through panel_hle_emit(), which checks there is
room for the PAIR and drops the whole message if not. The MIDI key path did
not: it called enqueue_fast_pad_byte() twice, and that function drops a single
byte when the queue is full.

So one full queue at the wrong moment puts the command byte in, loses the code,
and every message after it is read one byte out of step - the command of the
next event is taken as this event's keycode, and so on. That is not a dropped
keypress, it is a lasting corruption that turns one key into another, and it
matches the reported symptom exactly: pressing RESTART sometimes acting as STOP
or AFTER, intermittently, with no pattern.

Measured on the appliance beforehand, with the hub's own trace merged against
the machine's lamp export on a common clock: every key PRESS the hub sent was
correct at the wire, and the wrong key still fired. The controller and its
mapping were never at fault.

Honest about what is and is not proven: the asymmetry above is certain, and so
is its consequence. What is NOT confirmed is that the queue actually filled on
this appliance - no overflow warning was ever logged. The warning fires only on
a completely full queue and says nothing about the framing damage, which is why
it also now counts the drops and says plainly that the stream is out of step.
If the symptom survives this, that counter is the thing to read.

diff --git a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
--- a/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
+++ b/src/mame/akai/mpc2000.cpp
@@ -1091,7 +1091,16 @@
 
 	if (m_fast_pad_count == m_fast_pad_queue.size())
 	{
-		osd_printf_warning("MPC fast-pad input queue overflow; dropping byte %02x\n", data);
+		// Dropping ONE byte of a two-byte message desynchronises the panel
+		// stream for every message after it, so this is not a lost keypress -
+		// it is a lasting corruption. Callers should use panel_hle_emit(),
+		// which reserves the pair. Counted as well as warned, because a
+		// warning that scrolls past is how this went unnoticed.
+		m_panel_hle_drops++;
+		osd_printf_warning(
+				"MPC fast-pad input queue overflow; dropping byte %02x "
+				"(%u dropped) - THE PANEL STREAM IS NOW OUT OF STEP\n",
+				data, m_panel_hle_drops);
 		return;
 	}
 
@@ -1181,8 +1190,16 @@
 	{
 		const uint8_t code = uint8_t(m_midi_note - 52 + 1);
 		const bool down = (kind == 0x90) && data;
-		enqueue_fast_pad_byte(down ? 0x84 : 0x85);
-		enqueue_fast_pad_byte(code);
+		// panel_hle_emit(), not two bare enqueues.
+		//
+		// A panel key event is TWO bytes and the receiver has no way to
+		// resynchronise if it gets one. enqueue_fast_pad_byte() drops a single
+		// byte when the queue is full, so this path could put the command in
+		// and lose the code - and from then on every following event is read
+		// one byte out of step, turning one key into another. panel_hle_emit()
+		// checks for room for the pair and drops the whole message instead,
+		// which every other panel message already does.
+		panel_hle_emit(down ? 0x84 : 0x85, code);
 		return;
 	}
 
